<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Paul Rubens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1577">1577</a>–<a href="/years/1640">1640</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Paul Rubens was the leading painter of the Flemish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> in Nassau, where his parents lived in exile. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he ran a large workshop in Antwerp producing altarpieces, portraits and mythological scenes for the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and served as a diplomat for the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, negotiating peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1629–1630.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens.jpg" alt="Peter Paul Rubens" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Self-portrait_-_RH.S.180_-_Rubenshuis_(after_restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rubens was born on 28 June 1577 to Jan Rubens and Maria Pypelincks, whose family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. His father, a lawyer, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, and in 1568 the family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, fearing persecution under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>. After Jan Rubens died in 1587 the family moved to Antwerp, where Peter Paul studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and classical literature. In 1590 he had to start working, and after serving as a page to a countess he began an apprenticeship with the landscape painter Tobias Verhaecht in 1592.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1600 Rubens travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> he saw paintings by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, Veronese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tintoretto">Tintoretto</a>. He entered the service of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-i-gonzaga">Vincenzo I Gonzaga</a> of Mantua, painting portraits of the ducal family and copying works in his collection, and in 1601 went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he studied classical sculpture and the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> and Leonardo. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1608, and in September 1609 was appointed court painter by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-vii-archduke-of-austria">Archduke Albert</a> and the Infanta Isabella Clara Eugenia, with permission to keep his studio in Antwerp. He married Isabella Brant on 3 October 1609, and from 1610 he lived and worked in a villa he designed, now the Rubenshuis Museum. His pupils and collaborators included Anthony van Dyck.</p><p>In 1621 Marie de' Medici commissioned him to paint two cycles for the Luxembourg Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the one on her own life was installed in 1625. After the Twelve Years' Truce ended in 1621 the Spanish Habsburg rulers entrusted him with diplomatic missions, and between 1627 and 1630 he travelled between the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to seek peace between the Spanish Netherlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. Philip IV of Spain raised him to the nobility in 1624, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> gave him an honorary degree in 1629, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a> knighted him in 1630. His output was large: Michael Jaffé's catalogue lists 1,403 works, not counting the many copies made in his workshop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1630 Rubens, whose first wife Isabella had died four years earlier, married the 16-year-old Helena Fourment, who became the model for the full-figured women of many of his paintings of the 1630s. In 1635 he bought the Steen, an estate outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, and painted landscapes there. He died on 30 May 1640 of heart failure resulting from chronic gout and was buried in Saint James' Church in Antwerp, where a family chapel was completed in 1650 with an altarpiece he painted himself. He is considered the most influential artist of the Flemish Baroque, and the terms Rubensian and Rubenesque are derived from his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Paul Rubens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sustained much of the art of the Low Countries: Rogier van der Weyden was city painter of Brussels, Dieric Bouts worked in Leuven, Pieter Bruegel the Elder lived in Antwerp and Brussels, and <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> made Antwerp the centre of Flemish Baroque painting in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The princely family’s art collection, one of the most important private collections in Europe, includes major works by <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> and Frans Hals and is shown in the family’s Garden Palace and City Palace in Vienna; sales from it after the Second World War, when the family…</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In music Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina became the model for Catholic composers; in art its painters and sculptors included El Greco, <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> and Bernini.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Siegen was the birthplace of <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> in 1577, when his parents lived there in exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-nassau-siegen">Principality of Nassau-Siegen</a></li><li><blockquote>The provinces became a Counter-Reformation stronghold; <b>Rubens</b> worked in Antwerp.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.02&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Siegen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4732 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Paul Rubens"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5182 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5182 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.02&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
